1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Korea carved it in stone — and
printed it on money.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
This may be the most widely printed star
chart in the world.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
On its back: a whole sky of 1,467 stars
in one circle.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
A chart of the heavens, carved in stone
in 1395 for King Taejo of Joseon.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Tradition says it was recut from a
Goguryeo-era Pyongyang rubbing.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Korea calls it the world’s second-oldest
stone star chart.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
If you know the Chinese sky, it’s the
same asterisms — and the same names.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Look high in the east: three bright
stars along the Milky Way.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
In the West: Vega.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
In the West: Altair.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
In the West: Deneb. Together, the Summer
Triangle.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Written in the same Chinese characters,
but read in Korean: Jingnyeo, Hago,
Cheonjin.

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
From Goguryeo to Joseon to today’s
wallets: the sky hasn’t changed — only
the voices reading it.

14
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Qixi: the Cowherd and the Weaver.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

